@startuml
title Validating a transaction — restricted onchain check versus a client-side proof
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #4A78B5
skinparam ParticipantBackgroundColor #EEF5FF
skinparam ParticipantBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam ActorBackgroundColor #EEF5FF
skinparam ActorBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam LifelineBorderColor #4A78B5
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #F5F9FF
skinparam noteBorderColor #4A78B5

actor User
participant "Client\n/ wallet" as C
participant "Bundler\nor sequencer" as B
participant "EntryPoint\nor kernel" as E
participant "Account\ncontract" as A

group ERC-4337
  User -> C : sign a UserOperation
  C -> B : submit to the alt mempool
  B -> A : simulate validateUserOp
  note over B, A #F5F9FF
    The bundler runs the account's own code
    and must be protected from it: ERC-7562
    forbids most opcodes, restricts storage
    access, and caps validation gas.
  end note
  B -> E : handleOps(bundle)
  E -> A : validateUserOp
  A --> E : validation data
  E -> A : execute the call
  note over E #F5F9FF
    Validation runs onchain, so its cost
    is paid on Ethereum and scales with
    how complex the account's rules are.
  end note
end

group Aztec
  User -> C : approve the action
  C -> A : run the entrypoint locally
  A -> A : authenticate however\nthe contract defines
  C -> C : prove the whole execution\nin the kernel circuits
  note over C #F5F9FF
    Validation never leaves the device.
    Verifying 100 signatures costs the
    user proving time and costs the
    network nothing.
  end note
  C -> B : proof + side effects
  B -> B : verify a constant-size proof
  note over B #F5F9FF
    No restriction on validation logic is
    needed, because the sequencer never
    executes it.
  end note
end
@enduml
